1
00:00:33,436 --> 00:00:37,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have a few suttas that I would like to go through.

2
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:38,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's...

3
00:00:41,021 --> 00:00:42,903
[SPEAKER_01]: This theme is really about sense restraint.

4
00:00:45,695 --> 00:00:53,841
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha gave a short teaching to Bahiya.

5
00:00:54,422 --> 00:00:57,184
[SPEAKER_01]: He gave a short teaching to Malonkeya Buddha.

6
00:00:58,605 --> 00:00:58,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

7
00:00:59,786 --> 00:01:00,467
[SPEAKER_01]: The same verse.

8
00:01:06,552 --> 00:01:09,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll just read out Nyanaviru's translation of this.

9
00:01:12,285 --> 00:01:12,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

10
00:01:12,925 --> 00:01:15,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, bahir, you should train your... train thus.

11
00:01:16,327 --> 00:01:18,208
[SPEAKER_01]: In the seen, there shall be just the seen.

12
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:20,710
[SPEAKER_01]: In the heard, there shall be just the heard.

13
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:23,091
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sensed, there shall be just the sensed.

14
00:01:24,052 --> 00:01:26,253
[SPEAKER_01]: In the cognized, there shall be just the cognized.

15
00:01:27,014 --> 00:01:29,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, bahir, should you train yourself.

16
00:01:31,016 --> 00:01:40,702
[SPEAKER_01]: When, bahir, for you, in the seen, there shall be just the seen, the heard, just the sensed, just the cognized, then you will not

17
00:01:41,746 --> 00:01:42,826
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when he says,

18
00:02:09,688 --> 00:02:18,579
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not be that by which... you will not be in that place, and you'll not be here or any longer.

19
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:22,663
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think, here, yonder, and between the two.

20
00:02:22,784 --> 00:02:23,785
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that, you know?

21
00:02:24,626 --> 00:02:28,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Here is the sense basis.

22
00:02:30,131 --> 00:02:31,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yeah.

23
00:02:31,472 --> 00:02:33,153
[SPEAKER_01]: You will neither be your sense places.

24
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:42,843
[SPEAKER_00]: But you neither... Yeah, yourself will neither be taken to be your sense places, or sense objects, or both, or neither, whichever.

25
00:02:42,863 --> 00:02:47,147
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, sometimes you can, through denial of things, you can establish your sense of self.

26
00:02:48,908 --> 00:02:49,729
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can deny

27
00:02:51,537 --> 00:03:04,845
[SPEAKER_00]: There is here, there is yonder, and in between the two, would stand basically for both here and yonder, and neither here nor yonder, the mental attitude in regard to the experience as a whole of here and yonder.

28
00:03:05,506 --> 00:03:13,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the self can always kind of, even through denial of it, can re-establish itself as not that, but implying I am something else.

29
00:03:14,891 --> 00:03:17,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Although there isn't anything else to be found simply through that denial.

30
00:03:18,645 --> 00:03:23,467
[SPEAKER_00]: negation of something, you give enough space for the notion of self to remain.

31
00:03:24,767 --> 00:03:33,131
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, basically, when in seeing, there is just the seeing, no amount of

32
00:03:34,612 --> 00:03:52,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Movement, so to speak, of the mind, in regard to the I and the sights, and by the experience of the seen, no matter the movement, whether it's in it, apart from it, both in it or apart from it, whether it's mine, whether it's for me, whether it's this, none of that will induce the sense of self.

33
00:03:53,897 --> 00:03:56,017
[SPEAKER_00]: When seen, there is just the seeing.

34
00:03:57,017 --> 00:04:03,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, in other words, when the seeing is not appropriate, when the senses that are seeing are not appropriate,

35
00:04:04,016 --> 00:04:07,499
[SPEAKER_00]: When both, the senses and that which is being seen, are not appropriate.

36
00:04:07,939 --> 00:04:09,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, in the seen, there's just the seen.

37
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:21,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're neither appropriating it, and appropriation is basically, eh... established either through indulging it, or running away from it.

38
00:04:22,269 --> 00:04:24,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Indulging it, or denying it.

39
00:04:26,652 --> 00:04:30,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you basically relate to it, so to speak.

40
00:04:31,095 --> 00:04:33,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And that relation is a form of appropriation.

41
00:04:35,403 --> 00:04:41,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why he doesn't... he doesn't either indulge the sights, nor does he fear them or run away from them.

42
00:04:42,327 --> 00:04:43,687
[SPEAKER_00]: The scene is just a scene.

43
00:04:43,748 --> 00:04:46,749
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no need to do anything in regard to it, for and of.

44
00:04:49,911 --> 00:04:53,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have a pleasing sight or a displeasing sight.

45
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:53,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

46
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:58,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasing sight is just a pleasing sight.

47
00:04:58,501 --> 00:05:05,804
[SPEAKER_00]: For somebody who is not developed in regard to pleasing sight, pleasing sight would be inducing lust in their mind.

48
00:05:06,024 --> 00:05:07,805
[SPEAKER_00]: The lust is not in the pleasing sight.

49
00:05:08,625 --> 00:05:09,466
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

50
00:05:09,486 --> 00:05:16,008
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another of Nyanavira's translations in the back of the book, saying that the target perceives beautiful objects in the world.

51
00:05:16,689 --> 00:05:24,872
[SPEAKER_00]: He still sees, oh, this is beautiful, this is agreeable, this is nice, but his thoughts do not have desire and lust in regard to those things.

52
00:05:27,375 --> 00:05:35,481
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's when people can't see that, can't see that the nature of desire and lust pertains to the mind, it's not in the objects, then they deny the objects.

53
00:05:35,822 --> 00:05:41,706
[SPEAKER_00]: They try to overcome desire and lust through sheer denial of objects.

54
00:05:41,767 --> 00:05:50,854
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the sutta, the simile of the bait, in Majjhima Nikaya, where Cho, one deer herd, eats Mara's bait, i.e.

55
00:05:50,954 --> 00:05:52,255
[SPEAKER_00]: enjoys the senses carelessly.

56
00:05:53,113 --> 00:05:58,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, because of that, Māra does with them what he wants.

57
00:05:58,437 --> 00:06:00,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, another group of deer sees that.

58
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:06,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Sees that the hunter controls this deer, does what he wants, because they're eating their traps unwarily.

59
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:08,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they think, okay.

60
00:06:09,907 --> 00:06:19,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in, in

61
00:06:23,164 --> 00:06:25,551
[SPEAKER_00]: They deny the entire sense sphere.

62
00:06:27,998 --> 00:06:31,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And in that sutta says the deer then shuns the bait completely.

63
00:06:32,364 --> 00:06:34,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Forgetting that they do depend on it to some extent.

64
00:06:35,646 --> 00:06:43,052
[SPEAKER_00]: They shine, they go up in the mountains, but then, sooner or later, the food, the water runs out in the mountains, and they have to come back to their bed.

65
00:06:43,272 --> 00:06:49,636
[SPEAKER_00]: And because they were never developed in regard to the bed in the first place, they'll end up eating it carelessly, like the first day ahead.

66
00:06:49,697 --> 00:06:52,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, in the end, Mara does with them what he wants.

67
00:06:53,119 --> 00:06:56,862
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly like the indulgence and denial results in the same.

68
00:06:58,158 --> 00:07:04,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Results in you not being... not understanding the nature of debate and being controlled because of that.

69
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:08,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is that, eh...

70
00:07:09,962 --> 00:07:11,983
[SPEAKER_00]: The deer, herd, would recognize that.

71
00:07:12,343 --> 00:07:16,644
[SPEAKER_00]: It would recognize the nature of the indulgence, the nature of denial.

72
00:07:17,444 --> 00:07:22,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it would stay within the domain of bait, because it depends on a certain extent of it.

73
00:07:22,725 --> 00:07:23,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, it depends on food.

74
00:07:24,606 --> 00:07:26,286
[SPEAKER_00]: But it would not eat carelessly.

75
00:07:26,926 --> 00:07:29,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the key.

76
00:07:29,667 --> 00:07:29,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

77
00:07:30,307 --> 00:07:31,367
[SPEAKER_00]: It would eat carefully.

78
00:07:31,628 --> 00:07:33,048
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be careful of the bait.

79
00:07:33,961 --> 00:07:40,262
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you wouldn't deny everything, you wouldn't throw away the baby with the bathwater, it would basically start distinguishing.

80
00:07:41,122 --> 00:07:51,904
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a very... well, it's not necessarily subtle, if the mind starts getting used to it, but for most people, it's a very subtle distinction, because people either say yes or no completely.

81
00:07:53,425 --> 00:07:57,465
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, obviously, you have to start with restraint first.

82
00:07:57,705 --> 00:08:00,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Restraint in regard to things that are not necessary.

83
00:08:04,319 --> 00:08:11,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Such as sexual integrals, things based on excessive lust, things based on anger and so on.

84
00:08:11,843 --> 00:08:14,544
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, eating food is necessary.

85
00:08:15,864 --> 00:08:17,405
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can do it lustfully.

86
00:08:18,145 --> 00:08:19,646
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do it because of craving.

87
00:08:20,387 --> 00:08:21,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you can do it mindfully.

88
00:08:22,007 --> 00:08:27,370
[SPEAKER_00]: But people who don't know, who can't distinguish that the lust is on them, not on the food,

89
00:08:28,191 --> 00:08:29,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they start denying food.

90
00:08:29,432 --> 00:08:30,212
[SPEAKER_00]: They try void food.

91
00:08:30,333 --> 00:08:31,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he says, that was happening.

92
00:08:31,413 --> 00:08:32,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Asceticism.

93
00:08:32,654 --> 00:08:33,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-mortification.

94
00:08:33,755 --> 00:08:36,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that will... sooner or later, that won't be enough.

95
00:08:37,238 --> 00:08:38,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they start denying more.

96
00:08:38,338 --> 00:08:42,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it can go as far as denying breathing, denying air, denying anything.

97
00:08:43,542 --> 00:08:46,044
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha tried that and he said, no, this is not the way.

98
00:08:47,685 --> 00:08:51,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that doesn't mean that, okay, so now you should go back and eat everything unwarily again.

99
00:08:52,529 --> 00:08:53,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And be completely unrestrained.

100
00:08:53,930 --> 00:08:56,952
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's... that's as silly as, you know, as it can be.

101
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:01,321
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha was quite clear how one should then practice.

102
00:09:01,941 --> 00:09:08,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you develop the virtue first, you develop the strength, you stop doing unwholesome things that are completely unnecessary and they have... like inexcusable.

103
00:09:09,063 --> 00:09:16,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, other things, like, you know, you won't have... you can't help by encountering agreeable sights, agreeable sounds.

104
00:09:17,905 --> 00:09:27,589
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're just blaming sights and sounds, well, the Buddha again said, you won't be different than a blind or a deaf man, if you think that the domain of sight, domain of sound, is the problem.

105
00:09:27,729 --> 00:09:28,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

106
00:09:28,510 --> 00:09:30,390
[SPEAKER_00]: It's desire and lust in regard to it.

107
00:09:31,211 --> 00:09:45,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of being the second deer that runs to the mountain in fear of the whole domain, no, you recognize that, so you stay within it, practice restraining to the extent necessary, but then, for the rest, you start developing mindfulness in regard to it.

108
00:09:45,537 --> 00:09:47,638
[SPEAKER_00]: That means eating the food carefully.

109
00:09:48,424 --> 00:09:50,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being careless in regard to the bait.

110
00:09:51,365 --> 00:09:52,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the bait is.

111
00:09:52,806 --> 00:09:53,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Carelessness.

112
00:09:53,726 --> 00:09:56,807
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what gives rise to lust, to aversion, to delusion.

113
00:09:57,067 --> 00:09:58,067
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the actual food.

114
00:09:58,308 --> 00:09:59,688
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the actual sense objects.

115
00:10:00,208 --> 00:10:01,489
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how agreeable they are.

116
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:10,132
[SPEAKER_00]: What was that other quote you just read earlier on today?

117
00:10:10,232 --> 00:10:11,052
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll read it, yeah.

118
00:10:11,272 --> 00:10:13,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, read it now, because it's exactly what I'm saying.

119
00:10:16,288 --> 00:10:23,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Just before that, what you're saying now, because to shun the bait, seems to me, is a

120
00:10:25,197 --> 00:10:27,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there some sort of ill will there?

121
00:10:27,999 --> 00:10:28,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, of course.

122
00:10:28,579 --> 00:10:29,220
[SPEAKER_01]: You're pushing away.

123
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:29,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

124
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:31,001
[SPEAKER_00]: It's aversion.

125
00:10:31,021 --> 00:10:31,581
[SPEAKER_01]: You're aversion.

126
00:10:31,761 --> 00:10:33,662
[SPEAKER_00]: You're averse towards that pleasing stuff.

127
00:10:33,682 --> 00:10:34,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of fear.

128
00:10:34,202 --> 00:10:38,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you fear that agreeable sight, sound, smell, taste, touch will induce lust.

129
00:10:38,505 --> 00:10:41,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you absolutely have no no recourse to prevent it.

130
00:10:41,867 --> 00:10:43,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't understand it.

131
00:10:43,948 --> 00:10:51,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way that you know how to get... push things away, the ill will... Well, you push away pleasing things and then

132
00:10:52,637 --> 00:10:54,738
[SPEAKER_01]: You bring the things that displease you closer.

133
00:10:54,938 --> 00:10:55,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

134
00:10:55,699 --> 00:10:56,099
[SPEAKER_01]: That hurts.

135
00:10:56,119 --> 00:10:57,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Then that becomes that which pleases you.

136
00:10:57,820 --> 00:11:01,261
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up just acting in the same way like the first herd.

137
00:11:03,262 --> 00:11:11,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's... the other herd, they go... they see what the hunt is about, they see what the bait is about, and they've overcome him.

138
00:11:12,447 --> 00:11:13,707
[SPEAKER_01]: He's kind of bewildered by...

139
00:11:13,747 --> 00:11:19,250
[SPEAKER_00]: They stay there, but they don't eat the bait, so he can't see them, because they're never caught on the hooks.

140
00:11:20,555 --> 00:11:22,838
[SPEAKER_00]: But the food is eaten around the bed.

141
00:11:23,058 --> 00:11:24,199
[SPEAKER_01]: But is there really a bed?

142
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:26,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Or does the answer really exist?

143
00:11:26,982 --> 00:11:28,003
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an illusion.

144
00:11:29,025 --> 00:11:33,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's not... That is the case.

145
00:11:33,790 --> 00:11:35,272
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the situation which you're born in.

146
00:11:36,312 --> 00:11:39,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, that's all the gospel.

147
00:11:39,395 --> 00:11:45,220
[SPEAKER_00]: People can deny it through all sorts of views and stuff, which is, of course, that's why the acknowledgment of those things has to come first.

148
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:48,383
[SPEAKER_00]: But what we are talking about, we assume that we agree on that.

149
00:11:48,743 --> 00:11:52,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a different topic now.

150
00:11:52,967 --> 00:11:59,153
[SPEAKER_00]: We agree that there is such thing as being controlled by the senses and being a victim to it.

151
00:12:10,893 --> 00:12:19,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is another sutta from Samyutta Nikaya and the chapter 35, verse 94.

152
00:12:21,322 --> 00:12:25,185
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called Untamed or Unguarded.

153
00:12:25,205 --> 00:12:28,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... the Buddha speaking.

154
00:12:29,088 --> 00:12:36,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Because these six bases for contact, if untamed, unguarded, unprotected, unrestrained, are bringers of suffering.

155
00:12:42,585 --> 00:12:49,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Just six bhikkhus are the basis for contact, where one unrestrained meets with suffering.

156
00:12:49,908 --> 00:12:54,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Those who know how to restrain them dwell uncorrupted with faith they partner.

157
00:12:57,490 --> 00:13:05,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Having seen forms that delight the mind, and having seen those that give no delight, dispel the path of lust towards the delightful.

158
00:13:07,202 --> 00:13:16,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Having seen the delightful, it doesn't say run away from delightful.

159
00:13:17,084 --> 00:13:18,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Cover your eyes from delightful.

160
00:13:19,784 --> 00:13:22,605
[SPEAKER_00]: It says dispel the lust in regard to delightful.

161
00:13:24,545 --> 00:13:30,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Having seen a non-delightful, don't end up thinking about how non-delightful... i.e.

162
00:13:30,086 --> 00:13:33,887
[SPEAKER_00]: don't end up evolving... developing aversion.

163
00:13:34,643 --> 00:13:45,429
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to non-delightful, mental aversion, but it doesn't say run away from the non-delightful because, you know, that is the way out.

164
00:13:45,609 --> 00:13:46,009
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

165
00:13:46,630 --> 00:13:50,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you're blaming non-delightful and you're blaming delightful for your lust and for your aversion.

166
00:13:51,072 --> 00:13:52,713
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing it where it is.

167
00:13:53,493 --> 00:13:56,155
[SPEAKER_00]: By not seeing it where it is, you cannot possibly uproot it.

168
00:14:04,732 --> 00:14:16,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Having heard sounds both pleasant and noisy, do not be enthralled by pleasant sound, dispel the course of hate towards the noise, and do not soil the mind by thinking, this one is displeasing to me.

169
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:30,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Having smelt a fragrant, delightful scent, and having smelt a putrid stench, dispel aversion towards the stench, and do not yield to desire for the lovely.

170
00:14:32,590 --> 00:14:40,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Having enjoyed a sweet, delicious taste, and having sometimes tasted what is bitter, do not greedily enjoy the sweet taste.

171
00:14:41,334 --> 00:14:43,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Do not feel aversion towards the bitter.

172
00:14:45,796 --> 00:14:47,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it can't be clearer than that.

173
00:14:47,977 --> 00:14:50,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, many people blame the taste.

174
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:54,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Do not greedily enjoy the sweet taste.

175
00:14:54,901 --> 00:14:56,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't be greed in regard to...

176
00:14:56,522 --> 00:14:59,143
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean don't ever eat sweet, agreeable taste.

177
00:15:01,304 --> 00:15:01,464
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

178
00:15:02,165 --> 00:15:08,107
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're genuinely equanimous in regard to the taste, it doesn't matter if it's agreeable or disagreeable.

179
00:15:09,688 --> 00:15:17,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, if you're genuinely equanimous, you won't be running away from agreeable, because you fear that it will induce the lust.

180
00:15:17,610 --> 00:15:17,770
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

181
00:15:18,311 --> 00:15:22,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Equanimity is basically absence of lust for understanding.

182
00:15:23,352 --> 00:15:30,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, removing of greed in regard to the agreeable, in regard to delicious, having enjoyed delicious sweet taste.

183
00:15:31,608 --> 00:15:35,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can even enjoy it, in a way, without being greedy about it.

184
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:36,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

185
00:15:36,721 --> 00:15:37,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's agreeable.

186
00:15:37,803 --> 00:15:39,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a form of enjoyment.

187
00:15:40,717 --> 00:15:41,637
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just a fact.

188
00:15:42,038 --> 00:15:43,198
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense enjoys it.

189
00:15:43,798 --> 00:15:45,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't necessarily enjoy it.

190
00:15:45,919 --> 00:15:48,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's obvious that many people don't enjoy it because they fear it.

191
00:15:49,020 --> 00:15:49,881
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a mistake.

192
00:15:50,341 --> 00:15:51,661
[SPEAKER_00]: So you let the sense enjoy it.

193
00:15:51,681 --> 00:15:51,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

194
00:15:51,841 --> 00:15:52,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have to eat.

195
00:15:52,582 --> 00:15:58,264
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't do it unwarily, you don't do it carelessly, because that will then induce greed in your mind.

196
00:15:58,324 --> 00:16:01,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you will be appropriating sense that enjoys it.

197
00:16:01,725 --> 00:16:03,986
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't enjoy the taste even if you want to.

198
00:16:04,646 --> 00:16:05,727
[SPEAKER_00]: It always pertains to the

199
00:16:06,687 --> 00:16:12,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Like that physical sense, presence of the matter, that is your tongue.

200
00:16:13,912 --> 00:16:25,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fact that you think that you can enjoy it, and then you deny it, through running away, through practicing extreme asceticism, through not eating, because, you know, you fear the taste, basically.

201
00:16:26,899 --> 00:16:32,323
[SPEAKER_00]: You are fully appropriating your sense base, and then protecting it, because you can't let go of it.

202
00:16:33,077 --> 00:16:34,878
[SPEAKER_01]: The hunter's chasing you, kind of thing.

203
00:16:35,018 --> 00:16:35,678
[SPEAKER_01]: You've been chased.

204
00:16:35,778 --> 00:16:36,878
[SPEAKER_01]: You're running away.

205
00:16:37,619 --> 00:16:40,900
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't sort of... Well, no, yeah.

206
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:46,622
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, in a sense, you can't run away, because you're holding on to the bait.

207
00:16:46,782 --> 00:16:47,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh.

208
00:16:47,382 --> 00:16:53,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, OK. You're not eating it, because you're fearing it, but you're holding on to it, which amounts to the same thing.

209
00:16:53,404 --> 00:16:56,125
[SPEAKER_00]: You're hooked by the bait, one way or the other.

210
00:16:57,066 --> 00:17:00,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Either through eating it carelessly, or through, like, holding it tightly out of fear.

211
00:17:02,436 --> 00:17:04,017
[SPEAKER_01]: We've seen it as well.

212
00:17:04,618 --> 00:17:11,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can never see that actually you can't even enjoy the taste, even if you want to, that the sense will enjoy the taste, for as long as you deny it.

213
00:17:12,363 --> 00:17:14,144
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as you deny agreeable taste.

214
00:17:15,725 --> 00:17:17,606
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know for a fact that you're restrained.

215
00:17:17,866 --> 00:17:19,488
[SPEAKER_00]: You know for a fact that you eat once a day.

216
00:17:20,108 --> 00:17:23,110
[SPEAKER_00]: You know for a fact that you're not seeking out for a pleasant taste.

217
00:17:23,430 --> 00:17:25,972
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get... you encounter a pleasant taste.

218
00:17:26,712 --> 00:17:30,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And feeling that is just basically complete lack of wisdom.

219
00:17:30,956 --> 00:17:33,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Running away from it, it's even further lack of wisdom.

220
00:17:34,039 --> 00:17:39,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to prevent it, trying to not feel it with your tongue or whatever, is just plain ridiculous.

221
00:17:41,187 --> 00:17:43,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those objects or the senses

222
00:17:45,345 --> 00:17:49,587
[SPEAKER_01]: For the senses, in their domain, it's the kind of... it's their business, kind of.

223
00:17:49,647 --> 00:17:50,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

224
00:17:50,267 --> 00:17:51,068
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever they get...

225
00:17:51,088 --> 00:17:53,128
[SPEAKER_00]: The five animals, they have feeding grounds.

226
00:17:53,469 --> 00:17:54,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not...

227
00:17:54,769 --> 00:18:00,652
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you will be affected by it when you appropriate that feeding ground, when you appropriate that animal that feeds them.

228
00:18:00,952 --> 00:18:12,277
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are... when your mind is inseparable from your tongue, when your tongue is appropriated, when your eyes are appropriated, you will be... well, you will be affected by what you see, what you taste, and so on.

229
00:18:13,243 --> 00:18:16,684
[SPEAKER_00]: And running away from it is just another form of being affected by it.

230
00:18:21,625 --> 00:18:27,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no matter what comes into the eye, so to speak, no matter what you see...

231
00:18:27,607 --> 00:18:30,607
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, in the scene, there is a scene, and then you will not be dead by which.

232
00:18:31,448 --> 00:18:32,488
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what it means.

233
00:18:33,368 --> 00:18:40,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Being dead by which, in another sutta, when Ananda says, it's the eye, eye, as the organ, makes you dead, by which you are the conceiver.

234
00:18:41,718 --> 00:18:43,260
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who sees the things.

235
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:45,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you end up conceiving them as mine.

236
00:18:46,443 --> 00:18:52,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because you just automatically appropriated the sense bases, because they don't... they don't appear as objects.

237
00:18:52,990 --> 00:18:55,814
[SPEAKER_00]: They're always negative in regard to it, which means they're even harder to see.

238
00:18:57,375 --> 00:18:59,135
[SPEAKER_00]: They have to be seen as a negative phenomenon.

239
00:18:59,836 --> 00:19:01,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, most people don't see it.

240
00:19:01,376 --> 00:19:11,778
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, because of their appropriation of their sense base, in this case, let's say, eyes, they are the seer of the eye objects.

241
00:19:12,319 --> 00:19:14,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they just descended down into the eye.

242
00:19:14,799 --> 00:19:15,379
[SPEAKER_00]: They took that.

243
00:19:16,059 --> 00:19:19,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, what I see, means I see that.

244
00:19:19,580 --> 00:19:22,601
[SPEAKER_00]: What my tongue tastes, I'm tasting that.

245
00:19:23,518 --> 00:19:25,159
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's to mean they're being that by which.

246
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:32,084
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if seen, there is just the seen, in taste, there's just the taste, they will not be that by which.

247
00:19:32,584 --> 00:19:37,788
[SPEAKER_00]: When they're not that by which, when they don't appropriate the sense organs, well, they will not be in the objects.

248
00:19:38,408 --> 00:19:46,114
[SPEAKER_00]: When they're not appropriating sense organs, nor are they found in the objects, they will neither be here, nor yonder, nor between the two.

249
00:19:47,203 --> 00:19:49,784
[SPEAKER_00]: No behind, in front, above, left or right.

250
00:19:50,204 --> 00:19:53,025
[SPEAKER_00]: There will simply not be room for the sense of self.

251
00:19:53,485 --> 00:20:01,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sense of self cannot arise without the basis which is appropriation, regarding things as mind.

252
00:20:02,067 --> 00:20:09,809
[SPEAKER_00]: When that's completely prevented, through understanding that it's impossible to be there in the first place, you can't own those things in the first place,

253
00:20:11,137 --> 00:20:13,760
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you prevent the appropriation, means there is no room for sense of self.

254
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:16,662
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even think, oh, now I have to practice the non-self.

255
00:20:16,702 --> 00:20:16,843
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

256
00:20:16,883 --> 00:20:20,566
[SPEAKER_00]: That experience of non-appropriation is the experience of non-self.

257
00:20:24,089 --> 00:20:29,014
[SPEAKER_01]: We take, like, there's some of those animals, and the senses as the animals.

258
00:20:29,694 --> 00:20:33,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the dog is eating its food, but if you

259
00:20:35,297 --> 00:20:39,898
[SPEAKER_01]: You can never have that food that the dog is eating, because you're not... you're not the dog.

260
00:20:40,078 --> 00:20:40,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not the dog, yes.

261
00:20:40,898 --> 00:20:51,420
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you thought you were holding on to the dog, you know, and thinking that you are eating this food, whatever it is, but that's... you can't get any of that stuff.

262
00:20:51,440 --> 00:20:53,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're going to be suffering constantly.

263
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:55,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, think of it like this, basically.

264
00:20:55,541 --> 00:21:02,722
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way for you to eat the food is for tongue, mouth, digestive organ to engage with the food.

265
00:21:04,467 --> 00:21:09,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And all of those things, if you look at them independently, so to speak, are independent of you.

266
00:21:10,029 --> 00:21:12,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mouth, your organ, everything.

267
00:21:12,269 --> 00:21:14,310
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's not in your control.

268
00:21:14,390 --> 00:21:20,172
[SPEAKER_00]: But if those things have arisen and they correspond... well, they have objects that correspond to it, they're engaged with it.

269
00:21:21,352 --> 00:21:29,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not recognizing the impermanence of the sense bases, for example, you appropriate it automatically.

270
00:21:29,255 --> 00:21:30,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the similar... the animals...

271
00:21:31,667 --> 00:21:36,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't quite work in this sense, because you can always... well, nothing stops you from eating dog food.

272
00:21:36,910 --> 00:21:42,553
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is, there are five animals, but basically, you know the domains because of them.

273
00:21:43,653 --> 00:21:48,376
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't... you can't have... you cannot have a relationship with sight without the eyes.

274
00:21:49,356 --> 00:21:57,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, basically, all you can have a relationship with is... all you can have a relationship with is eyes seeing things.

275
00:21:58,761 --> 00:22:00,282
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't access sights.

276
00:22:01,606 --> 00:22:03,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless I see them first.

277
00:22:04,968 --> 00:22:06,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't even have the idea.

278
00:22:06,109 --> 00:22:10,031
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even mentally think about sights without implying eyes.

279
00:22:11,032 --> 00:22:19,077
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't appropriate your eye, then your eye is going to just look at... it's going to be unrestrained.

280
00:22:20,017 --> 00:22:20,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

281
00:22:20,397 --> 00:22:21,138
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people think.

282
00:22:21,218 --> 00:22:21,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

283
00:22:21,998 --> 00:22:24,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they think, oh, so I must let go of my eyes, which means...

284
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:27,962
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, you're still... you are responsible for restraint.

285
00:22:29,207 --> 00:22:52,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you start restraining, and the precepts are quite clear, whether 5, whether 8, whether 10, no matter how many, they're quite clear, and that's what you decided beforehand, and then you start seeing the negative phenomenon of the eye, eye object, sorry, eye base, eye basis, the sense basis of the eyes, and then you recognize, okay, so it's still within that restraint.

286
00:22:53,658 --> 00:22:58,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were now to make the jump from non-appropriation of the eye to a non-restraint of the eye,

287
00:22:58,826 --> 00:23:00,527
[SPEAKER_00]: That would have to be a conscious jump.

288
00:23:01,707 --> 00:23:03,008
[SPEAKER_00]: That can't happen by accident.

289
00:23:03,108 --> 00:23:03,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

290
00:23:03,308 --> 00:23:10,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Because eyes stopped engaging in unwholesome, fully unwholesome things through setting up the sīla first.

291
00:23:12,213 --> 00:23:14,474
[SPEAKER_00]: So now you can genuinely let go of the eyes.

292
00:23:14,574 --> 00:23:14,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

293
00:23:14,934 --> 00:23:16,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they won't go into that unwholesome.

294
00:23:16,355 --> 00:23:19,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they used to go into unwholesome only because you are sending them there.

295
00:23:22,278 --> 00:23:22,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, really.

296
00:23:22,758 --> 00:23:25,800
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha said you can't possibly engage in sensuality

297
00:23:27,125 --> 00:23:31,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having fully established sensual perceptions.

298
00:23:31,327 --> 00:23:32,828
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that, you're fully responsible.

299
00:23:36,129 --> 00:23:39,131
[SPEAKER_00]: And that cannot be done without appropriation of the eyes.

300
00:23:39,571 --> 00:23:42,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, quite the opposite from what people believe.

301
00:23:43,073 --> 00:23:52,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Genuinely letting go, genuinely not appropriating the eye base, fully prevents even a possibility of sensual engagement.

302
00:23:56,572 --> 00:24:00,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is intentional, once the eyes have been taken as mine.

303
00:24:01,770 --> 00:24:06,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you relish the life, the pleasure that you fully feel you can access.

304
00:24:06,311 --> 00:24:07,852
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point of sensuality.

305
00:24:07,892 --> 00:24:09,312
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole nature of it.

306
00:24:09,832 --> 00:24:13,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel you're in touch with your disagreeable objects.

307
00:24:14,493 --> 00:24:19,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Either through consuming them through your mouth, through your eyes, through your nose, through grasping them with your hands.

308
00:24:19,995 --> 00:24:21,315
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly what sensuality is.

309
00:24:21,355 --> 00:24:21,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Consumption.

310
00:24:22,696 --> 00:24:31,478
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only assume that you can consume things when you have appropriated basis that they're the only ones that can access them.

311
00:24:32,631 --> 00:24:45,681
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, all you're consuming, assuming that you're consuming, are the sense bases that then, on their own external kind of level, correspond to these sense objects and interact with them.

312
00:24:46,101 --> 00:24:48,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can never actually access the sights.

313
00:24:49,324 --> 00:24:54,227
[SPEAKER_00]: All you can have is... all you can see, so to speak, is I seeing things.

314
00:24:55,108 --> 00:24:55,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Already doing it.

315
00:24:55,788 --> 00:24:56,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

316
00:24:56,189 --> 00:25:00,212
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you can have is your sense base being affected by these things, but you can't access those things.

317
00:25:01,490 --> 00:25:02,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't actually taste the food.

318
00:25:03,371 --> 00:25:06,052
[SPEAKER_00]: You can taste your tongue tasting the food.

319
00:25:06,833 --> 00:25:07,873
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't touch things.

320
00:25:08,754 --> 00:25:13,296
[SPEAKER_00]: You can experience your body being touched by things or your body touching things.

321
00:25:13,496 --> 00:25:14,957
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, it's always indirect.

322
00:25:15,517 --> 00:25:23,601
[SPEAKER_00]: You can never bypass the body as a sense of touch or bypass the tongue and go straight to the sense objects, which means the sense objects

323
00:25:24,545 --> 00:25:27,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Our color is always determined by your sense base.

324
00:25:28,327 --> 00:25:30,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if your eyes are not seeing certain colors, that's it.

325
00:25:30,829 --> 00:25:32,249
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even conceive those colors.

326
00:25:33,210 --> 00:25:38,533
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even conceive... Only like in some... like maybe external communication might get the idea that you're seeing things differently.

327
00:25:38,553 --> 00:25:39,714
[SPEAKER_00]: But for you, that is what you see.

328
00:25:39,734 --> 00:25:40,534
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the real thing.

329
00:25:41,975 --> 00:25:46,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you can only access senses that are being affected by external objects, by external matter.

330
00:25:48,359 --> 00:25:51,941
[SPEAKER_00]: And that recognition already uproots any desire you can have.

331
00:25:54,376 --> 00:25:55,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

332
00:25:55,237 --> 00:25:57,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're inaccessible.

333
00:25:57,719 --> 00:26:04,444
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how hard you press, how hard you squeeze, how hard you taste, how hard you look, you know, you're fully understood.

334
00:26:05,085 --> 00:26:07,807
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't doubt it anymore that all you're seeing is your eyes seeing.

335
00:26:08,488 --> 00:26:09,829
[SPEAKER_00]: All you're tasting is your tongue tasting.

336
00:26:11,130 --> 00:26:12,671
[SPEAKER_00]: All you're touching is your body touching.

337
00:26:15,053 --> 00:26:16,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's always an indirect.

338
00:26:17,316 --> 00:26:18,096
[SPEAKER_00]: An indirect manner.

339
00:26:18,116 --> 00:26:21,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't turn away, turn back and look at the things.

340
00:26:21,538 --> 00:26:23,940
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way you can see them is by having a mirror in front of you.

341
00:26:26,321 --> 00:26:27,482
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your way of looking back.

342
00:26:29,583 --> 00:26:32,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this simile, looking back is basically the sense objects.

343
00:26:33,005 --> 00:26:35,106
[SPEAKER_00]: They are out there in the world, inaccessible.

344
00:26:35,526 --> 00:26:38,548
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to access them is when they are reflected upon the sense base.

345
00:26:40,248 --> 00:26:43,450
[SPEAKER_00]: When they engage, there's a corresponding engagement with the sense base.

346
00:26:43,790 --> 00:26:44,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you see it.

347
00:26:46,574 --> 00:26:48,074
[SPEAKER_00]: How it corresponded to it.

348
00:26:48,535 --> 00:26:52,656
[SPEAKER_00]: But it still doesn't mean you're actually accessing the matter of the sense objects.

349
00:26:52,816 --> 00:26:53,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

350
00:26:54,176 --> 00:26:57,977
[SPEAKER_00]: They will require you to come out of all of your sense bases and then go straight to the matter.

351
00:26:58,437 --> 00:26:59,518
[SPEAKER_00]: But that is inconceivable.

352
00:26:59,858 --> 00:27:03,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even that thought is based on a complete experience of sense bases.

353
00:27:04,739 --> 00:27:05,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Even imaginary bases.

354
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:07,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in your imagination.

355
00:27:13,722 --> 00:27:13,922
[SPEAKER_01]: In a

356
00:27:14,880 --> 00:27:16,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Six... something with the six animals.

357
00:27:18,041 --> 00:27:18,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Five.

358
00:27:18,862 --> 00:27:19,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Six... five.

359
00:27:20,983 --> 00:27:22,643
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember what they are.

360
00:27:22,783 --> 00:27:23,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Crocodile, human.

361
00:27:23,544 --> 00:27:24,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Crocodile, monkey.

362
00:27:24,344 --> 00:27:24,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I forgot.

363
00:27:25,745 --> 00:27:26,865
[SPEAKER_01]: And they... Is it six?

364
00:27:27,906 --> 00:27:30,827
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's five animals and then the mind, where they meet in the mind.

365
00:27:31,107 --> 00:27:31,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

366
00:27:31,527 --> 00:27:32,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the five senses.

367
00:27:32,307 --> 00:27:34,228
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's six because it's the monkey.

368
00:27:34,949 --> 00:27:36,649
[SPEAKER_01]: He says the monkey is the mind.

369
00:27:36,669 --> 00:27:37,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe.

370
00:27:37,370 --> 00:27:37,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way.

371
00:27:37,870 --> 00:27:38,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway.

372
00:27:38,772 --> 00:27:40,813
[SPEAKER_01]: They they pull to their own feeding grounds.

373
00:27:41,033 --> 00:27:43,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And the thing is, he's got a... there's a rope tied.

374
00:27:43,775 --> 00:27:46,756
[SPEAKER_01]: He ties the rope in the middle, and then they just pull.

375
00:27:47,277 --> 00:27:50,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And whoever's ever the strongest, that's where everyone else will go.

376
00:27:50,358 --> 00:27:52,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Everyone else goes, yeah, because they're tied to the same, yeah.

377
00:27:52,439 --> 00:27:54,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So everyone goes where the crocodile falls, or whatever.

378
00:27:57,002 --> 00:28:00,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he doesn't just tie it in the middle, he ties it to a post.

379
00:28:01,004 --> 00:28:02,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's discernment, that's mindfulness.

380
00:28:03,628 --> 00:28:04,448
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, they get tired.

381
00:28:05,049 --> 00:28:05,809
[SPEAKER_01]: They stop pulling it.

382
00:28:05,869 --> 00:28:08,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they can't actually go there, because they're tired.

383
00:28:08,230 --> 00:28:13,873
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that's similar to the fact that, like, you don't... you don't tie it in the middle together, because then the strongest will pull.

384
00:28:14,214 --> 00:28:17,875
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically when people use a certain sense to overcome other senses.

385
00:28:20,157 --> 00:28:28,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, unpleasant sights, sounds are bothering you, but then your mind just goes... starts thinking about pleasant taste.

386
00:28:29,181 --> 00:28:32,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you're both... you're bothered by unpleasant touch, unpleasant taste.

387
00:28:32,897 --> 00:28:37,441
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you light a beautiful incense, and then you're enjoying a pleasant smell, and your mind calms down because of that.

388
00:28:38,061 --> 00:28:39,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

389
00:28:39,763 --> 00:28:59,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Or sometimes admiring the beautiful view as a form of meditation, which is as mistaken as it can be, because it's exactly, ah, this sense seems calmer than the others, so you use that sense to overcome the others, but then that sense, when that sense changes, you will be pulled, and all the other senses will go for it, because this is the strongest among them.

390
00:28:59,220 --> 00:29:00,461
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't really mean going that way.

391
00:29:01,787 --> 00:29:06,030
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you must not use any sense as a basis for meditation, because that's not meditation.

392
00:29:06,710 --> 00:29:08,031
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha did say that, actually.

393
00:29:08,892 --> 00:29:19,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a jackal waiting for a mouse, or an owl waiting for a prey, preying upon a fish, like waiting, hunting, hunting, waiting, grabbing the moment, waiting, waiting, waiting.

394
00:29:19,078 --> 00:29:25,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So the misguided person over-meditates, under-meditates, mis-meditates, pre-meditates, but he does meditate.

395
00:29:26,991 --> 00:29:33,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And basically, when they miss the point of meditation, they end up meditating with their mind revolving around sensuality.

396
00:29:34,358 --> 00:29:40,384
[SPEAKER_00]: And revolving around sensuality means revolving around dependence on a sense, for pleasure and for pain, peace.

397
00:29:43,967 --> 00:29:49,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, there is nothing pleasing in a sight, nothing pleasing in a smell, no pleasing, no taste, no...

398
00:29:49,718 --> 00:29:53,299
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a pleasing sight, but there is no greed for the pleasure of that sight.

399
00:29:53,379 --> 00:29:54,500
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a pleasing sight.

400
00:29:54,520 --> 00:30:00,481
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there's no pleasing... but there's... the only way you can get pleasure is through, eh... is how you embody.

401
00:30:00,962 --> 00:30:01,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

402
00:30:01,682 --> 00:30:02,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you will go...

403
00:30:02,462 --> 00:30:02,922
[SPEAKER_01]: In comfort.

404
00:30:03,302 --> 00:30:05,263
[SPEAKER_00]: You will go to the... Yeah, physical comfort, yeah.

405
00:30:07,208 --> 00:30:11,231
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's no physical contact, you go and fantasize about other sides, or even just go into your imagination.

406
00:30:11,631 --> 00:30:19,818
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of a reproduction of the other senses in their peaceful state, in the state of not being yanked by their hunger or whatever else.

407
00:30:20,398 --> 00:30:22,019
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's again, it revolves around them.

408
00:30:26,763 --> 00:30:33,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everyone's got a... well, I suppose everyone's more inclined to certain...

409
00:30:34,565 --> 00:30:35,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Senses.

410
00:30:35,305 --> 00:30:35,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

411
00:30:35,826 --> 00:30:40,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people are prone to be shy, or some people have issues with, like, food restraint, and so on.

412
00:30:41,368 --> 00:30:43,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Music, yeah, to listen to ear, yeah.

413
00:30:43,208 --> 00:30:44,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, that's, that's another thing.

414
00:30:44,349 --> 00:30:46,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, people listen to music as a form of meditation.

415
00:30:46,950 --> 00:30:48,790
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what I was describing now.

416
00:30:49,731 --> 00:30:52,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, it's, it's after, like, after the, after the misdrive.

417
00:30:57,930 --> 00:31:01,772
[SPEAKER_01]: When touched by a pleasant contact, do not be enthralled.

418
00:31:02,692 --> 00:31:04,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Do not tremble when touched by pain.

419
00:31:05,353 --> 00:31:12,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Look evenly on both the pleasant and the painful, not drawn or repelled by anything.

420
00:31:13,556 --> 00:31:19,299
[SPEAKER_01]: To me, that touch is quite a... it's very insight, you know, insight.

421
00:31:19,439 --> 00:31:19,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

422
00:31:21,099 --> 00:31:21,780
[SPEAKER_01]: It's so close.

423
00:31:21,840 --> 00:31:22,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Personal.

424
00:31:22,580 --> 00:31:24,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's quite a...

425
00:31:26,038 --> 00:31:29,221
[SPEAKER_01]: To be able to look evenly on both pleasant and painful touch.

426
00:31:33,063 --> 00:31:33,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

427
00:31:33,324 --> 00:31:45,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Not have your mind swayed by it, by the prospect of comfort, by the possibility of discomfort, if any physical kind.

428
00:31:46,614 --> 00:31:51,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Being swayed means not, not, not, not, not craving it and not running away from it.

429
00:31:54,020 --> 00:31:54,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

430
00:31:54,620 --> 00:31:56,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it wasn't here to begin with.

431
00:31:56,441 --> 00:31:57,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you running away from it?

432
00:31:59,623 --> 00:32:01,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you trying to avoid it?

433
00:32:01,803 --> 00:32:05,005
[SPEAKER_00]: You trying to avoid it means, basically, you're still regarding it as yours.

434
00:32:07,346 --> 00:32:11,908
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense bases, so you're trying to avoid things that correspond to your sense base.

435
00:32:12,168 --> 00:32:16,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're trying to to avoid comfort or discomfort, depending on your view.

436
00:32:17,250 --> 00:32:20,932
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will only be committed to avoiding it, because you're fully appropriating this body.

437
00:32:23,801 --> 00:32:33,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of letting go of the body, relinquishing any notion of appropriation, then sometimes comfort, sometimes discomfort.

438
00:32:35,031 --> 00:32:38,714
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind doesn't get swayed, doesn't get affected.

439
00:32:39,495 --> 00:32:44,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can feel that your whole body, well, you know, your whole body's touched.

440
00:32:44,579 --> 00:32:44,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

441
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:50,464
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's not appropriate, you know, you disapproach... See, that's the thing, you can't run away from that.

442
00:32:50,584 --> 00:32:51,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can't.

443
00:32:55,830 --> 00:33:03,834
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why mindfulness of the body plays such a fundamental... because of that, and because all the other senses are basically bodily.

444
00:33:05,234 --> 00:33:08,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all rooted, contained in this very body.

445
00:33:09,656 --> 00:33:21,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stop appropriating the body as a sense base, in this entirety, as a sixth sense base, then you won't be appropriating sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, or thoughts and images.

446
00:33:21,962 --> 00:33:24,123
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be appropriating anything, physically or mentally.

447
00:33:29,380 --> 00:33:31,321
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't appropriate it, you don't need to run away from it.

448
00:33:32,682 --> 00:33:35,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Because disagreeable... agreeable was not yours to begin with.

449
00:33:36,543 --> 00:33:40,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if there is any response to agreeable, it's a bodily response.

450
00:33:40,065 --> 00:33:44,207
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a response of the sense base that you don't appropriate, which means it's only a limited response.

451
00:33:44,867 --> 00:33:52,891
[SPEAKER_00]: It can never be a response with lust, a response with aversion, a response with delusion, because they're all intentional responses.

452
00:33:53,111 --> 00:33:57,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Responses for which you and your misappropriation of things was responsible for.

453
00:34:03,445 --> 00:34:05,706
[SPEAKER_01]: He continues further the same sutta.

454
00:34:06,947 --> 00:34:13,250
[SPEAKER_01]: When common people of proliferated perception deceive and proliferate, they become engaged.

455
00:34:14,030 --> 00:34:19,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Having dispelled every mind-state bound to the home life, one travels on the road of renunciation.

456
00:34:21,754 --> 00:34:26,717
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is thus well developed in six, these six phases,

457
00:34:27,538 --> 00:34:33,721
[SPEAKER_01]: If touched, one's mind never flutters anywhere, having vanquished both lust and hate.

458
00:34:34,581 --> 00:34:37,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhikkhus go to the far shore beyond birth and death.

459
00:34:42,965 --> 00:34:45,186
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is another sutta, the one after.

460
00:34:45,366 --> 00:34:46,886
[SPEAKER_01]: It's 95, Mulunkirt.

461
00:34:51,228 --> 00:34:52,529
[SPEAKER_01]: A great monk with a great name.

462
00:34:54,088 --> 00:34:57,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh, he goes to the... he's quite old, apparently, at this stage, and he...

463
00:34:58,190 --> 00:34:59,731
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I remember him from Manjulika.

464
00:34:59,751 --> 00:35:00,812
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a sotāpanna.

465
00:35:02,113 --> 00:35:02,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

466
00:35:02,593 --> 00:35:03,894
[SPEAKER_00]: A layman.

467
00:35:03,974 --> 00:35:04,334
[SPEAKER_00]: A layman.

468
00:35:05,575 --> 00:35:06,195
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a sotāpanna.

469
00:35:06,215 --> 00:35:12,078
[SPEAKER_00]: He was asking him, you know, how come sotāpanna's mind can sometimes also be affected by... by all sorts of things.

470
00:35:12,118 --> 00:35:13,899
[SPEAKER_00]: He forgets about dhamma, and so on.

471
00:35:14,560 --> 00:35:14,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

472
00:35:15,080 --> 00:35:15,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Also.

473
00:35:16,681 --> 00:35:17,762
[SPEAKER_00]: But maybe it was a different one.

474
00:35:17,782 --> 00:35:20,563
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if it was a different... yeah, but the same name.

475
00:35:20,923 --> 00:35:21,684
[SPEAKER_00]: The same name, yeah

476
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:30,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he asked the Buddha for a short... might... so, he might... a short discourse or short message.

477
00:35:30,963 --> 00:35:34,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he might dwell alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent, resolute.

478
00:35:47,310 --> 00:35:50,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha goes on to say, questions him.

479
00:35:50,352 --> 00:35:51,913
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, Malankeya Buddha?

480
00:35:52,533 --> 00:36:00,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any desire, lust or affection for those forms cognizable by the eye that you have not seen or never saw before?

481
00:36:00,458 --> 00:36:03,300
[SPEAKER_01]: That you do not see and would not think might be seen?

482
00:36:07,312 --> 00:36:29,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have any desire, lust or affection for those touches, cognizable by the body, that you haven't... have not touched you and never been touched by before, that you do... do not touch now and would not touch in the future?

483
00:36:31,064 --> 00:36:35,466
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible to have lust for things that have never

484
00:36:37,986 --> 00:36:39,507
[SPEAKER_00]: For sights, they have never been seen.

485
00:36:40,928 --> 00:36:42,389
[SPEAKER_00]: They will never be seen.

486
00:36:42,809 --> 00:36:44,050
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly imagine them.

487
00:36:44,630 --> 00:36:45,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, why?

488
00:36:45,410 --> 00:36:46,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they've never been seen.

489
00:36:53,615 --> 00:36:57,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't have lust for that which hasn't arisen.

490
00:37:03,281 --> 00:37:05,222
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have lust for that which

491
00:37:06,038 --> 00:37:11,461
[SPEAKER_00]: has not left an imprint on a sense base, so to speak.

492
00:37:12,101 --> 00:37:16,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Figuratively speaking, it isn't actually an imprint, but you can't have lust for that.

493
00:37:16,543 --> 00:37:17,303
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

494
00:37:18,824 --> 00:37:21,245
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's similar to what I was saying before.

495
00:37:22,506 --> 00:37:23,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that.

496
00:37:23,446 --> 00:37:33,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, if you see that, basically, it's impossible to lust or have aversion towards sight that has never been seen, never will be seen, you can't even imagine seeing.

497
00:37:35,702 --> 00:37:43,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you recognize that the sights that you did see, that you will see, are completely circumstantial.

498
00:37:44,965 --> 00:37:48,726
[SPEAKER_00]: They're far more inferior to all of the things that you haven't seen.

499
00:37:51,527 --> 00:37:51,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

500
00:37:51,887 --> 00:37:52,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they were seen.

501
00:37:52,688 --> 00:38:00,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always like things that are seen always arise on a basis of greater things that haven't been seen.

502
00:38:03,238 --> 00:38:16,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you realize that even your lust is completely perturbed and unnecessary and pathetic, because you're lusting this sight that was completely random and circumstantial and easily could have not been.

503
00:38:21,126 --> 00:38:22,007
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a big change.

504
00:38:22,247 --> 00:38:26,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you recognize that, in whatever you're experiencing, that this could have not been.

505
00:38:27,391 --> 00:38:28,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It is, but could have not been.

506
00:38:29,733 --> 00:38:35,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that it could have not been precedes the fact that it is, means, oh, it's completely circumstantial.

507
00:38:36,038 --> 00:38:37,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it was never for me.

508
00:38:38,079 --> 00:38:39,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It could have never been for me.

509
00:38:39,981 --> 00:38:41,962
[SPEAKER_00]: And it certainly will never be for me.

510
00:38:43,083 --> 00:38:49,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that being for me, being mine, it's already, like, makes it special, makes it, gives it priority.

511
00:38:51,254 --> 00:38:52,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes it more important.

512
00:38:52,635 --> 00:38:57,958
[SPEAKER_00]: But if something is circumstantial, ah, it could have not been, which means it won't be.

513
00:38:59,279 --> 00:39:00,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Something else becomes its place.

514
00:39:00,499 --> 00:39:02,721
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when that does, that could have also not been.

515
00:39:04,662 --> 00:39:07,043
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't have lust for things that have never been.

516
00:39:08,064 --> 00:39:15,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, when you do have lust for things that are, that's just on top of... that's completely gratuitous on top of that circumstantial arising.

517
00:39:16,783 --> 00:39:31,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just through recognizing that you can't have lust for things that are never... have never arisen, you can actually abandon lust for things that have, by seeing their circumstantial nature, which is nothing other than saying by seeing arisen things as impermanent.

518
00:39:32,138 --> 00:39:35,421
[SPEAKER_00]: And impermanent means impossible to own, means

519
00:39:37,030 --> 00:39:38,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, subject to cessation.

520
00:39:38,711 --> 00:39:39,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Subject to cessation.

521
00:39:40,251 --> 00:39:42,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you're actually witnessing their cessation.

522
00:39:43,392 --> 00:39:44,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You just recognize it.

523
00:39:44,212 --> 00:39:50,314
[SPEAKER_00]: This is circumstantial, which means you have no idea where it came from, and because of that, it will cease.

524
00:39:51,174 --> 00:39:54,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you keep witnessing it for the rest of your life, it still remains impermanent.

525
00:39:54,875 --> 00:39:57,196
[SPEAKER_01]: That covers everything that might be seen.

526
00:39:57,796 --> 00:40:03,958
[SPEAKER_00]: It covers all the possibilities as well, because whatever might be seen, it's going to be seen like this, on a circumstantial basis.

527
00:40:06,088 --> 00:40:07,028
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's the danger.

528
00:40:07,048 --> 00:40:10,250
[SPEAKER_01]: If you... if you don't cover it, if you don't know that...

529
00:40:10,370 --> 00:40:10,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

530
00:40:10,850 --> 00:40:14,771
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is... You left things unincluded, basically, in this reflection, yeah?

531
00:40:18,893 --> 00:40:23,574
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a... a couple, let's say, a man and a woman get together.

532
00:40:24,235 --> 00:40:24,935
[SPEAKER_01]: They love each other.

533
00:40:26,855 --> 00:40:28,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Infatuated with each other.

534
00:40:29,848 --> 00:40:36,630
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh, he's, you know, he's seen her, but he he might see someone else tomorrow.

535
00:40:37,471 --> 00:40:39,051
[SPEAKER_01]: There might be even more desire.

536
00:40:39,931 --> 00:40:43,093
[SPEAKER_01]: And he can't stop that from happening.

537
00:40:43,753 --> 00:40:47,034
[SPEAKER_00]: He can shut off his eyes, but then... Yeah, because you never practice restraint.

538
00:40:47,134 --> 00:40:50,655
[SPEAKER_00]: You go after things he sees, things he touches, things he pins his eyes.

539
00:40:51,035 --> 00:40:53,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he loses interest in his wife.

540
00:40:53,276 --> 00:40:53,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

541
00:40:53,636 --> 00:40:54,696
[SPEAKER_00]: He just moves on to another.

542
00:40:55,517 --> 00:40:58,938
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he were to see that even the first one was completely circumstantial,

543
00:41:01,436 --> 00:41:05,539
[SPEAKER_00]: He would have... well, he would have been able to control his desire, if not completely abandon it.

544
00:41:06,359 --> 00:41:07,540
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a reason for abandoning.

545
00:41:08,080 --> 00:41:24,491
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I'm saying is that even a layperson, not necessarily a monk who is practicing for arahantship, even a layperson, who is in the union with the opposite sex, can use this reflection to remain faithful, to remain restrained, to remain... to develop wisdom.

546
00:41:25,492 --> 00:41:30,435
[SPEAKER_00]: There's that sutta where the couple, the man and woman, came to the Buddha, and

547
00:41:31,353 --> 00:41:35,655
[SPEAKER_00]: And they asked him how to... and I think they were... they might both have the right view.

548
00:41:37,016 --> 00:41:40,817
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha... About how to be reborn together?

549
00:41:40,837 --> 00:41:49,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'm not sure if it was answered how to be reborn together, but it was basically sort of... he says, oh, yeah, they practice, like, perfect... they're perfectly faithful to each other.

550
00:41:49,081 --> 00:41:51,142
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, not just... they've never...

551
00:41:51,938 --> 00:42:04,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, husband says, not just that he never cheated on the wife, but he also never, never, never, like, verbally was unfaithful to her, and he was never even unfaithful to her, even in his mind.

552
00:42:05,746 --> 00:42:06,746
[SPEAKER_00]: And she says the same.

553
00:42:06,966 --> 00:42:09,448
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean that he never saw a prettier woman.

554
00:42:10,409 --> 00:42:24,501
[SPEAKER_00]: It simply means that they recognized, like, the intentional aspect of their desire, of their choice, and they clearly respected each other, and they just, okay, there was a more pleasing sight.

555
00:42:24,841 --> 00:42:27,783
[SPEAKER_00]: But more pleasing sight does not mean lust is there.

556
00:42:28,384 --> 00:42:32,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust is there when you're careless in regard to the more pleasing sight.

557
00:42:32,887 --> 00:42:33,808
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were never careless.

558
00:42:33,968 --> 00:42:35,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, they were never unfaithful to each other.

559
00:42:37,058 --> 00:42:37,718
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha praised it.

560
00:42:37,739 --> 00:42:40,580
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly how somebody who wants to be in union should live.

561
00:42:40,981 --> 00:42:49,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can see that's already quite a... quite a good degree of wisdom that most people today, an average person, just... it's inconceivable for them.

562
00:42:49,967 --> 00:42:50,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

563
00:42:50,227 --> 00:42:51,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what you see, you just go for it.

564
00:42:52,249 --> 00:42:59,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And then even though love, union, marriage, just remains utterly random and circumstantial based on just things.

565
00:42:59,474 --> 00:43:01,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yeah, people divorce.

566
00:43:01,175 --> 00:43:02,276
[SPEAKER_00]: People end up hating each other.

567
00:43:02,296 --> 00:43:02,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Because why?

568
00:43:02,756 --> 00:43:04,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they just follow the senses.

569
00:43:05,335 --> 00:43:09,036
[SPEAKER_00]: They are now either aging, or got bored with it, or found something better.

570
00:43:09,476 --> 00:43:14,176
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the whole kind of notion of marriage and commitment is sort of now basically hindering it.

571
00:43:14,697 --> 00:43:14,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

572
00:43:15,317 --> 00:43:18,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they feel enslaved by their own choices, basically.

573
00:43:19,577 --> 00:43:20,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And they only have themselves to blame.

574
00:43:21,398 --> 00:43:21,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Fantastic.

575
00:43:22,178 --> 00:43:22,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

576
00:43:26,018 --> 00:43:30,659
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this sutta, again, he goes into the... in the scene there, Shogun, just the scene.

577
00:43:30,959 --> 00:43:35,140
[SPEAKER_01]: He gives you that short teaching.

578
00:43:39,685 --> 00:43:47,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, Malankeya Putta, he says, I understand in detail, Venerable Sir, the meaning of what was stated by the Blessed One.

579
00:43:50,590 --> 00:43:52,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he gives, he says this verse.

580
00:43:55,892 --> 00:43:58,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha agrees with him.

581
00:43:58,053 --> 00:44:03,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Good, good, Malankeya Putta, it is good that you understand in detail the meaning of what was stated by me in brief.

582
00:44:04,796 --> 00:44:05,517
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just read down.

583
00:44:10,894 --> 00:44:17,658
[SPEAKER_01]: When seeing a sight, mindfulness becomes confused, if attention is focused on the pleasant aspect.

584
00:44:18,759 --> 00:44:24,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing it with a mind full of desire, one remains assuming to it, clinging to it, assuming.

585
00:44:26,263 --> 00:44:29,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Many feelings grow, arising from sights.

586
00:44:29,545 --> 00:44:40,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, back to the beginning of the verse, so it says, quite clearly, that wrongly attending to the pleasant sight,

587
00:44:41,913 --> 00:44:44,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire and lust and assumption grows in regard to it.

588
00:44:45,095 --> 00:44:48,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just by merely having a pleasant sight.

589
00:44:49,898 --> 00:44:53,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means pleasant sight or unpleasant sight was never the problem.

590
00:44:53,601 --> 00:44:54,421
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the problem.

591
00:44:55,682 --> 00:44:56,963
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you don't know

592
00:44:57,698 --> 00:45:01,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing how to attend to it, you end up being confused.

593
00:45:02,102 --> 00:45:03,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Being confused, you end up assuming.

594
00:45:04,104 --> 00:45:10,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming the whole desire, the whole... the whole, basically, thing ends up proliferating and growing more and more.

595
00:45:10,889 --> 00:45:11,990
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yeah, just...

596
00:45:12,030 --> 00:45:13,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Many feelings grow.

597
00:45:13,572 --> 00:45:15,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly, yeah.

598
00:45:15,773 --> 00:45:16,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Many...

599
00:45:16,874 --> 00:45:19,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Upon giving in to pleasure, you might sometimes experience more pain.

600
00:45:21,516 --> 00:45:26,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, by trying to run away from that pain, you discover some new pleasure.

601
00:45:26,297 --> 00:45:37,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was all based on the initial confusion in regard to pleasant side, which you, like, ignorantly craved for and went for, desired for, delighted in, lusted after.

602
00:45:38,920 --> 00:45:43,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, you just created basis for now all sorts of pleasant, unpleasant, neutral.

603
00:45:44,161 --> 00:45:46,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And giving into that, you create more basis for pleasant pleasure.

604
00:45:46,781 --> 00:45:48,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, feelings grow.

605
00:45:48,782 --> 00:45:49,162
[SPEAKER_00]: They just...

606
00:45:50,480 --> 00:45:53,302
[SPEAKER_01]: By cutting it off right there in that pleasant experience.

607
00:45:54,042 --> 00:46:16,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can take anything, no matter how proliferated it is, you can actually uproot it in its entirety, if you see the nature of craving towards pleasure, of nature, nature of avoiding, having aversion towards displeasure, the nature of indolence towards neither pleasure nor displeasure.

608
00:46:18,216 --> 00:46:19,697
[SPEAKER_00]: By understanding the nature of it,

609
00:46:20,562 --> 00:46:24,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that just pertains, permeates the whole, no matter how proliferated the picture is.

610
00:46:25,385 --> 00:46:30,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, basically, you kill the roots, you kill the proliferating life that was being fed by ignorance.

611
00:46:31,569 --> 00:46:33,591
[SPEAKER_00]: You dry it up, and then it's like a palm stump.

612
00:46:34,672 --> 00:46:39,035
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the proliferated tree branches remain petrified.

613
00:46:39,795 --> 00:46:40,736
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't spread anymore.

614
00:46:41,036 --> 00:46:42,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance doesn't fuel it.

615
00:46:42,597 --> 00:46:42,957
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

616
00:46:43,458 --> 00:46:49,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now it's just dried up habits of certain kind, the ones that were completely dependent upon sensuality, fall off.

617
00:46:50,515 --> 00:46:56,140
[SPEAKER_00]: But some of it remains, until the aggregates break apart completely, for another end.

618
00:47:00,144 --> 00:47:03,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You want someone's pure... obviously, you want feelings to grow, isn't it?

619
00:47:03,307 --> 00:47:06,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Like in the... the pleasure, do you want it to get more, more?

620
00:47:06,250 --> 00:47:06,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

621
00:47:06,751 --> 00:47:14,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's why... that's why, see, that's why sometimes people like to kind of expose themselves to the risk of this pleasure, because it makes that pleasure even greater.

622
00:47:15,378 --> 00:47:25,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within a pleasant, non-threatening situation, you kind of want to spice it up, so to speak, and then you make yourself kind of liable to some pain as well.

623
00:47:25,903 --> 00:47:31,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's all within the pleasurable image that you're already fully engaged with.

624
00:47:33,127 --> 00:47:34,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, overcoming challenges and stuff.

625
00:47:35,288 --> 00:47:38,569
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only engage that on the basis of pleasure.

626
00:47:39,510 --> 00:47:43,752
[SPEAKER_00]: If the whole thing suddenly turns, and then the whole thing becomes life-threatening,

627
00:47:44,698 --> 00:47:45,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, now it's serious.

628
00:47:47,199 --> 00:47:48,960
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you stop engaging in the same way.

629
00:47:50,260 --> 00:47:53,041
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, that's why people, you know, go after freedom, for example.

630
00:47:55,102 --> 00:48:00,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Even go after risk that, you know, makes them experience displeasure.

631
00:48:01,024 --> 00:48:07,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's all within the image of pleasure that they've initially given into and then proliferated.

632
00:48:08,669 --> 00:48:09,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, same.

633
00:48:09,989 --> 00:48:19,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, given into aversion in regard to this pleasure, even if you start experiencing pleasure on top of it, you won't be appreciating it, because, you know, the mind is upset, or whatever else.

634
00:48:19,793 --> 00:48:21,373
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feelings grow on top of each other.

635
00:48:22,033 --> 00:48:27,195
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you stop, establish mindfulness, all you can say is that a feeling is there.

636
00:48:27,355 --> 00:48:28,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

637
00:48:29,016 --> 00:48:32,557
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter if it's the top of the proliferated tree or the bottom.

638
00:48:32,597 --> 00:48:33,137
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

639
00:48:33,357 --> 00:48:35,978
[SPEAKER_00]: A feeling has arisen, which means every feeling

640
00:48:36,875 --> 00:48:42,177
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how many of them have grown, how many of them have spread, proliferated, got established, every feeling carries the nature of feeling.

641
00:48:42,857 --> 00:48:46,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, in regard to every feeling, you can practice abandoning of craving.

642
00:48:47,239 --> 00:48:51,461
[SPEAKER_00]: They all carry the principle, the nature, the universal principle, which this principle feeds on further.

643
00:48:51,481 --> 00:48:54,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

644
00:48:54,922 --> 00:48:57,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to chase all these proliferating feelings.

645
00:48:57,643 --> 00:48:58,404
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

646
00:49:00,745 --> 00:49:03,326
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, they're trying to trim all these branches that are constantly growing.

647
00:49:03,346 --> 00:49:04,366
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly keep up.

648
00:49:04,646 --> 00:49:06,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead, just kill the roots.

649
00:49:07,285 --> 00:49:07,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Uproot it.

650
00:49:08,826 --> 00:49:11,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Remove the food from the ground that the roots are sucking onto.

651
00:49:13,049 --> 00:49:13,429
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

652
00:49:13,529 --> 00:49:14,029
[SPEAKER_00]: They die.

653
00:49:14,169 --> 00:49:16,050
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to worry about the branches spreading anymore.

654
00:49:16,070 --> 00:49:17,251
[SPEAKER_00]: You know for sure they won't.

655
00:49:18,292 --> 00:49:21,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't know where exactly their proliferating got so big.

656
00:49:21,774 --> 00:49:27,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you know that you've killed the roots, one way or the other, and the tree's not being fed proliferation stuff.

657
00:49:34,602 --> 00:49:36,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, one task, you know.

658
00:49:36,807 --> 00:49:37,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

659
00:49:38,868 --> 00:49:46,512
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to trying to manage, contain, direct, fashion, the branches.

660
00:49:48,833 --> 00:49:49,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, manage.

661
00:49:50,534 --> 00:49:51,414
[SPEAKER_00]: It's form of management.

662
00:49:54,196 --> 00:49:56,697
[SPEAKER_01]: It is that many feelings grow, arising from sights.

663
00:49:57,257 --> 00:50:00,219
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is damaged by covetousness and cruelty.

664
00:50:01,099 --> 00:50:04,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Heaping up suffering like this is said to be far from nibbāna.

665
00:50:07,547 --> 00:50:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: When hearing a sound, mindfulness becomes confused.

666
00:50:10,188 --> 00:50:16,370
[SPEAKER_01]: If attention is focused on the pleasant aspect, experiencing with a mind full of desire, one remains assuming to it.

667
00:50:16,950 --> 00:50:19,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Many feelings grow, arising from sounds.

668
00:50:19,671 --> 00:50:22,712
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is damaged by covetousness and cruelty.

669
00:50:23,553 --> 00:50:26,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping up suffering like this is said to be far from nibbāna.

670
00:50:28,294 --> 00:50:34,917
[SPEAKER_01]: When smelling a smell, when savoring a taste, when touching a touch,

671
00:50:36,083 --> 00:50:46,651
[SPEAKER_01]: When knowing a mental phenomenon, it continues, you know, that mind is damaged and is far from nibbāna.

672
00:50:49,333 --> 00:50:54,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he goes on to say, seeing a sight with mindfulness, there is no desire for sights.

673
00:50:56,678 --> 00:51:01,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing it with the mind free of desire, one doesn't remain assuming to it.

674
00:51:02,802 --> 00:51:09,967
[SPEAKER_01]: As it is for someone who lives mindfully, when repeatedly seeing a sight, feeling is ended, not added to.

675
00:51:10,788 --> 00:51:14,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Reducing suffering like this is said to be in the presence of nibbāna.

676
00:51:15,131 --> 00:51:23,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So they see then, by seeing, by being mindful in regard to pleasant sight, mindful in regard to unpleasant sight, so he neither develops lust nor aversion.

677
00:51:24,558 --> 00:51:27,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindful in regard to neutral, neither agreeable nor disagreeable sight,

678
00:51:30,885 --> 00:51:34,606
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't develop indolence, he doesn't develop ignorance, he doesn't develop delusion.

679
00:51:35,887 --> 00:51:41,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means all those feelings that arise simultaneously with that, he understands as they arise.

680
00:51:41,888 --> 00:51:42,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't add to them.

681
00:51:43,289 --> 00:51:49,311
[SPEAKER_00]: He... and basically adding to them means he doesn't induce craving in regard to them.

682
00:51:51,151 --> 00:51:53,632
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what means ending of the feeling as it has arisen.

683
00:51:54,585 --> 00:52:07,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you create a feeling, obviously, you go further, and then you experience another feeling on account of that, and then another feeling on account of that, and it's just all piling up, so to speak, further, increasing.

684
00:52:08,013 --> 00:52:12,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're mindful in regard to sights, the feeling that has arisen, that's it.

685
00:52:13,196 --> 00:52:14,276
[SPEAKER_00]: You see it as impermanent.

686
00:52:14,857 --> 00:52:16,858
[SPEAKER_00]: By seeing it as impermanent, why?

687
00:52:16,918 --> 00:52:18,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're mindful in regard to sights,

688
00:52:19,932 --> 00:52:24,416
[SPEAKER_00]: There won't be any craving in regard to that feeling, because craving requires notion of permanence.

689
00:52:25,797 --> 00:52:28,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Be it craving for pleasure or craving for avoiding of pain.

690
00:52:29,420 --> 00:52:29,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Either of those.

691
00:52:30,941 --> 00:52:33,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by removing the craving, you stop.

692
00:52:33,664 --> 00:52:34,624
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop assuming it.

693
00:52:35,545 --> 00:52:36,346
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no upādāna.

694
00:52:41,170 --> 00:52:45,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Touching a touch with mindfulness, there's no desire for touches.

695
00:52:45,514 --> 00:52:48,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing with the mind fear of desire, one doesn't remain assuming to it.

696
00:52:49,197 --> 00:52:56,262
[SPEAKER_01]: As it is for someone who lives mindfully, when repeatedly touching a touch, feeling is ended, not added to.

697
00:52:56,463 --> 00:52:59,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Reducing suffering like this is said to be in the presence of nibbāna.

698
00:52:59,945 --> 00:53:06,491
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want to read that one out, because there's a difference between touch and feeling.

699
00:53:06,891 --> 00:53:08,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, absolutely.

700
00:53:09,922 --> 00:53:11,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I feel something in my back.

701
00:53:11,283 --> 00:53:13,103
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're touching something.

702
00:53:13,143 --> 00:53:13,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

703
00:53:14,084 --> 00:53:15,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a sensation in my knees.

704
00:53:15,845 --> 00:53:17,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that sensation is perception of the touch.

705
00:53:18,005 --> 00:53:18,946
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a feeling.

706
00:53:21,767 --> 00:53:23,348
[SPEAKER_00]: People think sensations are feelings.

707
00:53:23,368 --> 00:53:24,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensations are perceptions.

708
00:53:33,292 --> 00:53:38,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Like I said, repeatedly practicing mindfulness in regard to it, it uplifts it.

709
00:53:38,923 --> 00:53:39,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

710
00:53:39,503 --> 00:53:40,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling ends.

711
00:53:40,424 --> 00:53:41,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Reducing suffering like this.

712
00:53:41,924 --> 00:53:42,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

713
00:53:42,385 --> 00:53:43,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Reducing it.

714
00:53:43,165 --> 00:53:46,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Because suffering can arise only on the basis of craving.

715
00:53:47,287 --> 00:53:53,770
[SPEAKER_00]: And craving can arise only on the basis of ignorance in regard to feelings.

716
00:53:53,790 --> 00:54:01,394
[SPEAKER_00]: And ignorance in regard to feelings can arise only on the basis of non-mindfully attending to pleasure, non-mindfully attending to pain.

717
00:54:06,919 --> 00:54:08,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're touched by the feeling.

718
00:54:08,320 --> 00:54:10,502
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're touched by the feeling, you crave in regard to it.

719
00:54:12,944 --> 00:54:33,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by mindfully attending to what's seen, what's heard, what's tasted, what's touched, what's thought, by mindfully attending to it repetitively, the desire and lust in regard to it diminishes, aversion diminishes, which means that feelings are not entertained, not engaged, not craved for.

720
00:54:33,953 --> 00:54:39,435
[SPEAKER_01]: But each sight and so on is either agreeable or disagreeable.

721
00:54:40,355 --> 00:54:42,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, neither agreeable nor disagreeable.

722
00:54:42,055 --> 00:54:42,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's always felt.

723
00:54:43,856 --> 00:54:44,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

724
00:54:44,176 --> 00:54:46,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception and feeling are conjoined.

725
00:54:46,237 --> 00:54:48,417
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just have perception and feeling coming later.

726
00:54:48,897 --> 00:54:53,539
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always simultaneous.

727
00:54:53,559 --> 00:54:55,779
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the mind is determined by perception and feeling.

728
00:54:56,099 --> 00:54:57,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So experiencing an agreeable

729
00:54:59,472 --> 00:55:06,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can say there is an agreeable feeling corresponding to it, but that still doesn't mean that you're touched by pleasure.

730
00:55:07,677 --> 00:55:14,542
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're ignorant in regard to agreeable feeling, you basically... you're touched by pleasure, which means you crave for it.

731
00:55:18,524 --> 00:55:26,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And the craving is the inclination or the push towards... inclination towards... Yeah, I mean, it's subtler, like just leaning of the mind towards, leaning away.

732
00:55:28,547 --> 00:55:29,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Kravan for, Kravan against.

